course: everyday
lesson: 225
title: Good manners on buses and trains, and borrowing things
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Buses, trains and escalators are small shared spaces, so good manners there are mostly about making room and thinking of the people around you.
explain: Some people need a seat more than you do: older people, pregnant women, disabled people and people carrying small babies. Many buses and trains have priority seats near the doors just for them.
explain: Politeness never comes before your safety. If a stranger on a bus asks where you live or tries to get too friendly, you do not have to answer; move near the driver or another adult.
explain: Borrowing is the same idea at school: things belong to their owner, so you ask first, look after them, give them back, and own up if something goes wrong.
explain: Example: a girl in Toronto borrows her friend's new rubber and loses it. She tells her friend straight away, says sorry and buys a new one, and they stay friends.
fact: On a crowded bus or train, offer your seat to an older, pregnant or disabled person, because they may need it more than you.
fact: Many buses and trains have priority seats for people who find standing hard.
fact: A seat is for a person, so put your bag on your lap or the floor when the bus is busy.
fact: Eat smelly food before or after a journey, because strong smells bother people in a small space.
fact: Move away from the doors into the carriage so people can get on and off.
fact: On escalators in London and many big cities, people stand on the right and walk on the left, so people in a hurry can pass.
fact: You do not have to answer a stranger's personal questions; if you feel unsafe, move near the driver or another adult.
fact: When you have something new, like a game, invite your friend to share it instead of showing off.
fact: Always ask «May I borrow…?» and wait for a yes, even for a ruler or a rubber, because the thing is not yours.
fact: If you lose or break something you borrowed, tell the owner, say sorry and offer to replace it.
fact: If someone has not returned your thing, remind them kindly, and ask an adult if that does not work.
fact: Using a classmate's things while they are away from their desk is taking without asking.
exercise: The train is full and an old man with a walking stick gets on. What should you do? | Offer him your seat | Pretend to be asleep | Tell him to wait for the next train | Keep your seat because you got on first
exercise: Why should you offer your seat to a pregnant woman on a crowded bus? | She may need to sit more than you do | Pregnant women are not allowed to stand | The driver will fine you if you do not | It is only polite if she asks you first
exercise: What are priority seats on a bus for? | People who find standing hard, such as older or disabled people | The first people to get on the bus | Children travelling without a grown-up | People who have bought a more expensive ticket
exercise: The bus is getting busy and your school bag is on the seat next to you. What should you do? | Put the bag on your lap or the floor | Leave it, because you saw the seat first | Put your coat there as well | Ask people to stand so your bag stays dry
exercise: Why is it better not to eat a strong-smelling burger on a crowded bus? | The smell bothers people in a small space | Food is never allowed on any bus anywhere in the world | Burgers go cold faster on buses | The driver needs the food for later
exercise: You are standing by the doors of a crowded train. What is polite? | Move further inside to make room | Stay by the doors so you can get off quickly | Hold the doors open for your friends | Sit down on the floor by the doors
exercise: On an escalator in London, where should you stand? | On the right, leaving the left for people walking | On the left, leaving the right for people walking | In the middle, so nobody can pass | Anywhere, as long as you hold the rail
exercise: A stranger on the bus asks a boy where he lives and which school he goes to. What should he do? | Not answer, and move near the driver if he feels unsafe | Answer every question politely, because adults must be obeyed | Give a made-up address to be friendly | Get off at the next stop alone and walk home
exercise: You get a new video game and your friend has none. What is kind? | Invite your friend to play it with you | Tell everyone how much it cost | Play it in front of your friend without sharing | Say your friend's games are rubbish
exercise: You need a pencil and your friend has two. What should you do? | Ask «May I borrow your pencil?» and wait for a yes | Take one, since she has two | Take one and give it back later | Take it while she is not looking
exercise: You broke the calculator you borrowed from a classmate. What is right? | Tell him, say sorry and offer to replace it | Give it back and say nothing | Hide it and say you lost it | Say it was already broken
exercise: A friend borrowed your book weeks ago and has not given it back. What is a good first step? | Remind her kindly that you would like it back | Take something of hers until she returns it | Tell the whole class she is a thief | Never lend her anything again without asking why
exercise: Your classmate is out of the room and you need a ruler. What should you do? | Wait and ask her first, or borrow from someone who is there | Take hers, since she is not using it | Take it and put it back quietly before she comes back into the room | Take it, because a ruler is too small to matter
exercise: Why is showing off a new toy unkind? | It can make others feel left out and bad | It wears the toy out more quickly | Toys should never be taken to school | It is only unkind if the toy was expensive
test: A girl in Sydney is sitting in a priority seat on the train when a man with crutches gets on. What should she do? | Stand up and offer him the seat | Stay seated, since she is only a child | Move her bag onto another seat for him | Wait to see if anyone else stands first
test: A boy rides an escalator in a busy city station and stands on the left. A man behind him says «Excuse me». What should he do? | Move to the right so the man can walk past | Stand still where he is, as he was there first | Walk down the escalator the wrong way | Tell the man to use the stairs instead, since they are quicker
test: A girl gets on a full tram and stops just inside the door with her big backpack on. What would be better? | Move further in and hold her backpack low | Stay right by the door so that she is not squashed inside | Ask the people inside to get off | Put her backpack on an empty seat
test: A friendly woman on the bus asks a boy for his phone number and address. Which is true? | His safety comes before being polite, so he need not answer | It is rude to refuse an adult anything they ask for politely | He must answer because she was friendly | He should give her his parents' address and phone number instead
test: Sam borrows his friend's football and accidentally kicks it into a river. What should he do? | Tell his friend, say sorry and offer to replace it | Keep quiet and hope that his friend soon forgets all about it | Say someone else must have taken it | Buy a cheaper ball and say it is the same one
test: Why do classmates feel upset when someone uses their pens without asking? | The pens are theirs, and they may need them or wonder where they went | Pens break after being used by another person | Pens cost too much to share with anyone | Asking first is only needed with grown-ups
test: Your cousin has kept your skateboard for a month. You have reminded him twice. What next? | Ask a grown-up to help | Go to his house and take something of his | Shout at him in front of his friends | Give up and never mention it again
test: You have a packed lunch with eggs and tuna and a long bus ride ahead. What is considerate? | Eat it before or after the journey | Eat it on the bus with the window shut | Eat it quickly so the smell goes away | Share it with the passengers around you
test: bus is packed, old man with a stick just got on and i'm sitting. what do i do | Offer him your seat | Stay sitting, you got there first | Put your bag on the seat beside you | Pretend you didn't see him
test: forgot my ruler, jack's is right there on his desk n he's gone to the loo. can i just use it | No, wait and ask him first | Yes, a ruler is too small to matter | Yes, if you put it back quickly | Yes, he won't notice anyway
test: oops i broke the pen i borrowed off maya. what now | Tell her, say sorry and offer to replace it | Put it back in her pencil case and say nothing | Tell her it was already broken | Throw it away so she forgets
test: on the escalator in london, ppl keep saying excuse me behind me?? | Stand on the right so people can walk on the left | Stand in the middle so nobody pushes | Walk down the up escalator | Sit down on the step
explain: A girl in Toronto stood too close to the edge of a subway platform; a passing train's draught and the crowd's push made her stumble, and only a stranger grabbing her arm stopped her falling.
explain: A boy in Melbourne borrowed his friend's tablet and dropped it; he hid the crack and said nothing, and the friendship ended when his friend found out.
fact: The edge of a train platform is dangerous; people who fall onto the tracks can be killed by trains or by the electric rail, so you stand behind the yellow line and never go down onto the tracks, even for a dropped phone: you tell the staff.
fact: Strangers' private lives are their business: «snogging» is British slang for kissing, and «I'm done with him» means someone is ending a relationship.
fact: Damaging something you borrowed and hiding it usually costs more trust than the thing itself.
test: train's coming, wanna stand right at the edge to see | Stand back behind the yellow line | Lean out a bit to see it coming, it's fine | Stand on the edge, it's fun | Run along the edge
test: Why did hiding the crack in the borrowed tablet end the friendship? | His friend stopped trusting him | Tablets can never be fixed once they crack | Friends never lend things | The crack was too big
test: dropped my phone on the tracks | Don't go down, tell staff | Jump down and grab it | Lean down with a stick | Wait for the train to pass and get it
test: did u hear a kid was messing about near the edge of the train platform and fell on the tracks? real lesson? | Stay back from the platform edge; trains and the rail kill | Only stand near the edge when trains are slow | The tracks are safe when no train comes | Jump back up quickly if you fall
test: apparently that boy borrowed a girl's calculator, broke it and hid it, and now she won't speak to him ever. what's the true lesson? | Tell the owner, say sorry and offer to replace it | Hide it and buy a new one secretly | Say it was broken when you got it | Never borrow anything at all
test: my mate says some old lady on the bus told a kid off for not giving up his seat and he cried lol. should we spread it? | No, it's not ours to spread; offering a seat is kind though | Yes, it's a funny story | Yes, and say which school he goes to | Yes, old ladies always overreact
test: on the bus two teens behind you kiss and your little brother goes "EWWW they're snogging!!" loudly. what's best? | Quietly tell him it's their business and not to shout about people | Shout it louder so they stop | Take a photo to show everyone | Tell the driver to throw them off
test: lady on the train on her phone: "I'm done with him, I'm moving back to me mum's". what does she probably mean? | She is ending a relationship and moving back home | She has finished cooking dinner and is going out shopping now | She is done with her homework | Her mum is moving house
test: your friend borrowed your pen and gave it back with "Ben ❤️ Lucy" written on it. Lucy's in your class. what's right? | Rub it off and don't spread it, it's their private business | Show it to Lucy and the class at break | Write a mean word next to it | Give the pen to Ben's mum
exam: Identify the persons to whom one ought to offer one's seat on a crowded bus or train. | Older, pregnant or disabled persons | Only those persons who expressly request it | Only persons one knows personally | Only persons carrying bags
exam: Which of the following constitutes the appropriate manner of borrowing a ruler from a classmate? | To ask «May I borrow…?» and await consent | To take it quietly while the classmate is out of the room | To take it and return it later unasked | To take it if the classmate has two
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice on escalators in London and many large cities. | One stands on the right and walks on the left | One stands on the left and walks on the right | One stands in the middle of each step | One walks against the direction of travel
exam: Ascertain the proper course where a borrowed item has been lost or broken. | The owner is informed, an apology made and replacement offered | The matter is concealed from the owner | Another item is substituted secretly | The owner is blamed for lending it
